USER DEFINED FUNCTIONS (UDFs) - INTRODUCTION

What are UDFs?
- Similar to UDFs in other programming languages, such as Python
- MySQL UDFs do the following:
  - Accept parameter(s)
  - Perform action(s), such as a complex calculation
  - Return the result of the above action as a value

Why use UDFs?
- Ability to define functions not available directly in SQL
- Improves code readability

UDFs - EXAMPLE

Syntax:
/* Function Definition */
DELIMITER $$
CREATE FUNCTION function_name(func_parameter1, )
RETURNS datatype [characteristics]
BEGIN
  <MySQL statements>;
  RETURN expression;
END;
$$
DELIMITER ;

/* Function body must contain one RETURN statement */

Example:
/* Function Definition */
DELIMITER $$
CREATE FUNCTION age_today(release_date date)
RETURNS INT deterministic
BEGIN
  RETURN year(current_date) - year(release_date);
END;
$$
DELIMITER ;

/* Viewing Output */
SELECT 
  mov_id,
  movie_title,
  age_today(movie_main.release_date) AS movie_age
FROM movie_main;
